---
import ToolLayout from '../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
---
<ToolLayout
  tool="soil"
  intro="Enter the inside size of each bed and the depth of soil you want to add. You get the volume in cubic feet, cubic yards, litres and cubic metres, plus whole bags and cost if you add a bag size or price."
  related={[
    { id: 'spacing', why: 'once the bed is filled, see how many plants fit at the spacing on your seed packet.' },
    { id: 'mulch', why: 'work out a top layer of mulch for the same beds or the paths between them.' },
    { id: 'seed', why: 'turn seed packet timings into sowing dates for what you plan to grow.' },
  ]}
>
  <h2>How to measure a raised bed</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Measure inside the walls.</strong> Soil only fills the space between the boards, so measure the inside length and width. Thick timber or blocks can take several inches off the outside size.</li>
    <li><strong>For a round bed, measure the inside diameter</strong> straight across the middle, at the widest point.</li>
    <li><strong>For a corner bed, choose the triangular shape.</strong> Measure one straight side as the base, then the distance from the base to the far corner at a right angle. For a right-angled corner bed that is simply the other straight side.</li>
    <li><strong>Decide the depth of soil to add.</strong> This is not always the wall height. Most people stop a little below the rim so water and mulch stay in the bed.</li>
    <li><strong>Topping up?</strong> Measure from the current soil surface up to where you want the new surface, and enter only that depth.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Bed height and fill depth are different things</h3>
  <p>
    The wall height tells you how deep the bed could be filled. The fill depth is how much soil you are actually adding. A 12 inch (30 cm) bed filled to 2 inches below the rim needs 10 inches of soil, about 17% less than filling it to the brim. The calculator only ever uses the depth you enter. The optional wall height is there to flag a depth that is taller than the walls and to draw the side view.
  </p>

  <h2>Using the results</h2>
  <h3>Base volume and allowance</h3>
  <p>
    The base volume is the exact space you described. Loose soil and compost settle once watered, so some gardeners order a little extra. If you want that, enter an allowance as a percentage and it is shown as a separate line, so you can always see what the bed holds and what you added on top. No allowance is applied unless you enter one.
  </p>
  <h3>Bags bought together or per bed</h3>
  <p>
    With more than one bed there are two honest ways to count bags. <strong>Bought together</strong> adds all the beds first and rounds up once, which is what you need if you are happy to split a bag between beds. <strong>Rounded per bed</strong> rounds each bed up on its own, which suits beds filled at different times or with different mixes. The second number can never be lower than the first.
  </p>
  <h3>Comparing bag sizes</h3>
  <p>
    The bag that is cheapest each is not always cheapest overall, because the last bag is rarely full. Add up to three bag sizes with their prices, and a bulk price if you have one, and the comparison shows how many of each you would buy, how much would be left over, the total cost and the price per cubic foot or litre. The lowest cost is labelled in words. With the example figures, 22 bags of 2 cu ft at $10 ($220) beat 29 bags of 1.5 cu ft at $8 ($232).
  </p>
  <h3>Next: what to plant</h3>
  <p>
    Each rectangular bed in the results has a “Plan plants for this bed” link that opens the plant spacing calculator with the same length and width filled in.
  </p>
  <h3>Splitting a mix</h3>
  <p>
    If you blend your own soil, enter up to three ingredients and their parts, such as 2 parts topsoil to 1 part compost. The total volume is divided in those proportions, with a bag count for each ingredient if you entered a bag size. The recipe is yours: Prunest does not recommend one, because the right mix depends on your plants, climate and what is available locally.
  </p>
  <h3>Bulk soil</h3>
  <p>
    Bulk soil is usually sold by the cubic yard in the US and by the cubic metre (or bulk bag) elsewhere. The bulk estimate prices the exact volume. Suppliers round to their own delivery sizes, so treat it as a starting point for a quote.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="more">
    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <div class="worked">
      <p><strong>Two beds, filled with 1.5 cu ft bags, 10% extra for settling.</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Bed 1 is 8 ft × 4 ft inside, with 10 in of soil to add. 10 in is 10 ÷ 12 = 0.8333 ft, so 8 × 4 × 0.8333 = <strong>26.67 cu ft</strong>.</li>
        <li>Bed 2 is round, 4 ft across, with 12 in (1 ft) of soil. Radius is 2 ft, so π × 2² × 1 = <strong>12.57 cu ft</strong>.</li>
        <li>Base volume: 26.67 + 12.57 = <strong>39.23 cu ft</strong>.</li>
        <li>10% allowance: 3.92 cu ft, giving <strong>43.16 cu ft</strong> in total. That is 43.16 ÷ 27 = 1.598 cu yd, or about 1,222 litres (1.222 m³).</li>
        <li>Bags bought together: 43.16 ÷ 1.5 = 28.77, so <strong>29 bags</strong>.</li>
        <li>Rounded per bed: Bed 1 needs 29.33 ÷ 1.5 = 19.56, so 20 bags. Bed 2 needs 13.82 ÷ 1.5 = 9.22, so 10 bags. That is <strong>30 bags</strong>, one more than buying together.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Press “Try an example” with imperial units selected to load these numbers. This example is also one of the automated checks the calculator has to pass before each release.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>What this calculator does not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>No guessed weight.</strong> A cubic foot of soil can weigh very different amounts depending on the mix and how wet it is. Weight is shown only if you enter a density, in pounds per cubic foot or kilograms per cubic metre, from your supplier. For example, 27 cu ft at a stated 75 lb per cu ft is 27 × 75 = 2,025 lb.</li>
      <li><strong>No recommended mix or depth.</strong> How deep to fill, and with what, depends on what you grow and what is under the bed. Those choices are yours.</li>
      <li><strong>Mix volumes are simple shares.</strong> Fine and coarse ingredients pack together, so a blend can take up slightly less space than its parts measured separately.</li>
      <li><strong>Rectangles, circles and triangles.</strong> For an L-shaped bed, add it as two rectangles. <strong>Straight sides only.</strong> Beds with sloping sides, a base layer of logs or rubble, or an uneven floor will hold a different amount. Measure the depth of soil you are really adding.</li>
      <li><strong>Bag sizes vary.</strong> Some bags state a compressed volume or are sold by weight or dry quarts. Use the loose volume on the bag, in cubic feet or litres.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Unit reference</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>1 cubic yard = 27 cubic feet = 764.6 litres</li>
      <li>1 cubic metre = 1,000 litres = 35.31 cubic feet = 1.308 cubic yards</li>
      <li>1 cubic foot = 28.32 litres</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
<script>
  import '../tools/soil';
</script>
